<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<!-- GT_esmini verification asset (req-vd-ad:REQ-AD-026 step g / REQ-AD-030 step c,
     vd-func:FUNC-079 / FUNC-081; manualdrive_adas_verification_plan.md §3-1's
     MDA-XODR-01). Plain 500 m straight, TWO driving lanes per direction, identical
     in every respect to straight_500m_2lane.xodr EXCEPT that the posted speed limit
     STEPS DOWN part-way along the road.

       s in [0, 250)   -> 90 km/h  (25.0 m/s)
       s in [250, 500] -> 50 km/h  (13.888... m/s)

     WHY A STEP RATHER THAN A SECOND ROAD AT A DIFFERENT LIMIT: the observables this
     asset serves (gt.acc.effective_cap_mps, gt.msl.cap_mps) are judged as TIME
     SERIES with a visible discontinuity - "the cap followed the road" is a claim
     about a transition, and a constant limit cannot carry it. A run that merely
     started under some limit would pass any cap matcher that happened to be
     written for that number, whether or not the code ever reads the road at all.

     WHY 90 -> 50 AND NOT, SAY, 100 -> 80: the step (11.1 m/s) has to be larger than
     everything that could mask it - the ACC speed deadband (0.2 m/s), one setting
     step (1.39 m/s ~ 5 km/h) and the limiter's taper band (2.0 m/s) - so that a
     matcher can distinguish "the cap moved because the road changed" from "the cap
     moved because the driver pressed a button". It also brackets a comfortable
     driving speed on both sides, so a scenario can run at a fixed speed that is
     BELOW the first limit and ABOVE the second, which is what makes the same run
     serve the limiter's no-brake claim: the cap drops beneath the current speed,
     the throttle shuts, and the vehicle must NOT be braked back down.

     Deliberately NOT accompanied by a downhill-grade sibling. The verification plan
     originally called for one (MDA-XODR-02) to produce over-speed on a descent for
     REQ-AD-030 step a's negative, but RealVehicle applies road pitch to ATTITUDE
     ONLY - terrain_pitch_ never enters the longitudinal dynamics (RealVehicle.cpp)
     - so a graded road would look like a descent and behave like level ground.
     The asset would then be named for a phenomenon it cannot produce. The same
     claim is instead reached honestly here, via a cap that steps below the current
     speed. See the verification plan §3-1 for the record of that decision. -->
